  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q27-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "27",
    "prompt": "Twenty cards labelled 1 to 20 are shuffled, and two are dealt. Find the probability that their numbers:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q27-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "are both even",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{9}{38}$",
          "worked": "There are 10 even cards. The second card is drawn without replacement.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{both even})&=\\frac{10}{20}\\times\\frac{9}{19}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{both even})&=\\frac{9}{38}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q27-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "have an odd product",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{9}{38}$",
          "worked": "A product is odd only when both numbers are odd. There are 10 odd cards.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{odd product})&=\\frac{10}{20}\\times\\frac{9}{19}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{odd product})&=\\frac{9}{38}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q27-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "have an even sum",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{9}{19}$",
          "worked": "An even sum requires two even numbers or two odd numbers. These events are disjoint.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{even sum})&=\\frac{10}{20}\\times\\frac{9}{19}+\\frac{10}{20}\\times\\frac{9}{19}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{even sum})&=\\frac{180}{380}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{even sum})&=\\frac{9}{19}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q27-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "differ by one",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{1}{10}$\n\nCount the 19 consecutive pairs, each in either dealing order.",
          "worked": "There are $20\\times19=380$ equally likely ordered outcomes. The consecutive pairs are $(1,2),(2,3),\\ldots,(19,20)$: 19 pairs, each occurring in two orders.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{difference }1)&=\\frac{19\\times2}{20\\times19}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{difference }1)&=\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q27-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "add to a multiple of 10.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{9}{95}$\n\nCount distinct-card pairs with sums 10, 20 or 30.",
          "worked": "The possible multiples of 10 are 10, 20 and 30. Equal-number pairs are unavailable because each label occurs once.\n\nSum 10: $(1,9),(2,8),(3,7),(4,6)$, giving 4 pairs.\n\nSum 20: $(1,19),(2,18),\\ldots,(9,11)$, giving 9 pairs.\n\nSum 30: $(10,20),(11,19),(12,18),(13,17),(14,16)$, giving 5 pairs.\n\nEach pair occurs in two dealing orders.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{sum a multiple of }10)&=\\frac{2(4+9+5)}{20\\times19}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{sum a multiple of }10)&=\\frac{36}{380}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{sum a multiple of }10)&=\\frac{9}{95}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      }
    ]
  },
